data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_f731bbb06f6b499a8ee86813","messageId":"2e2a2f68-8303-48c3-9ef4-a291549e07fd","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ag-ui-frontend/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ag-ui-frontend\\n3: description: Use for frontend consumption of AG-UI messages, shared state, run status, executions, interrupts, and controlled Application Events, Frontend Tools, Agent-controlled frontend capabilities, and browser/client tool execution through project Runtime hooks.\\n4: compatibility: One AG-UI Agent Runtime per generated frontend; Phase 8 permits Plugin source writes while Runtime remains read-only.\\n5: allowed-tools: enable_creator_tools read_file ls glob grep edit_file\\n6: ---\\n7: \\n8: # AG-UI Frontend\\n9: \\n10: Maintain this data flow:\\n11: \\n12: ```text\\n13: AG-UI / Mock transport -> Agent Runtime -> Runtime Context Hooks -> UI Plugin -> project UI stack\\n14: ```\\n15: \\n16: ## Current project contract\\n17: \\n18: - `useAgentMessages()` returns normalized Agent messages, including user, assistant, reasoning, activity, and tool-related messages represented by Runtime Core.\\n19: - `useAgentState<TState>()` returns shared Agent state and must be narrowed safely before property access.\\n20: - `useAgentRun().status` is `idle`, `running`, `awaiting-input`, or `error`; a run error carries the user-visible failure detail.\\n21: - `useAgentExecutions()` exposes each Tool, Reasoning, Step, and Subagent lifecycle directly. Never replace local execution status with a generic Run loading flag.\\n22: - `useAgentInterrupts()` returns only current pending interrupts.\\n23: - `usePluginActions()` exposes instance-scoped Agent commands; `useAgentRuntimeActions()` exposes only Agent Runtime commands. Runtime-only UI state belongs in a named service or runtime store, not in AppUIModel composition.\\n24: - `usePluginEvents()` is the inbound, live-only Application Event channel. Subscribe only to names declared in the Plugin manifest and registered through the application's `Agent.appEventSchemas`.\\n25: - `usePluginService()` remains the component API for named Plugin capabilities.\\n26: - `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts` owns the explicit set of frontend capability operations advertised to the Agent.\\n27: \\n28: ## Boundaries\\n29: \\n30: - Do not instantiate an AG-UI client or manage a second Agent Runtime inside a Plugin.\\n31: - Do not invent a separate public message or state protocol when the Plugin Contract already exposes AG-UI data.\\n32: - Event, snapshot, delta, streaming, tool-call, and error normalization belongs in the frontend Runtime layer; Plugins render the normalized hook values.\\n33: - Never expose or import AG-UI `CUSTOM`, `CustomEvent`, `BaseEvent`, `RawEvent`, `rawEvent`, or `@ag-ui/core` from Plugin code. Preserve an explicitly supplied backend business event name and payload; never guess an unknown payload shape. Distinguish its consumers: a chat message renderer uses `defineDataMessageUI`, `definition.dataMessageUIs` and `manifest.data.messageUI`, and validates its raw `data`; a live Application Event subscriber uses `Agent.appEventSchemas`, `manifest.data.events` and `usePluginEvents`. Do not add a duplicate subscriber merely to render a chat message. Application schema transforms do not rewrite message data.\\n34: - For Application Event subscriptions, reuse an existing schema module; otherwise create an application-owned module relative to actual sourceRoot (for example `agent-contract/agent-events.ts`) and wire it through `Agent.appEventSchemas`. That directory is optional, not a preinstalled Runtime requirement. A Plugin-local schema wired through the application is also valid.\\n35: - Frontend Tools use standard AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `ToolMessage`, and a continuation Run. Never encode a frontend Tool call as `CUSTOM`, a new UserMessage, or a Plugin-specific protocol.\\n36: - The Agent calls a capability adapter, never React or a Plugin. Reuse or define a stable Service seam, let a Provider Plugin declare and provide that Service, then expose only the product-authorized operations in `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts`.\\n37: - Tool names use `lower_snake_case`; schemas use `z.strictObject(...)` as the single validation and JSON Schema source; handlers resolve Services at execution time and return short serializable results.\\n38: - Do not infer that every Service method should be exposed. Capability ownership and Agent exposure permission are separate decisions.\\n39: - Plugins must never self-register Frontend Tools or import AG-UI Tool types. They only provide capabilities through the existing Service lifecycle.\\n40: - Do not invent Application Events to replace standard Activity/Tool/Run lifecycle, persistent shared state, local Plugin communication, or frontend commands. An explicitly supplied business-job CUSTOM notification can drive a live panel through the application channel; it does not become standard Runtime execution progress or gain history/replay. A single Plugin's private projection may use component state and update by the supplied business ID. Subscribe in `useEffect` and return the unsubscribe function. Use existing shared state/services when persistence or cross-Plugin sharing is actually required.\\n41: - A request for different layout or placement is not an AG-UI Runtime change.\\n42: - During Phase 8, implement AG-UI presentation inside `/plugins/` while keeping Runtime and Framework source read-only.\\n43: \\n44: For interaction semantics, reason from product needs and AG-UI first. Treat external Agent UI projects as references, not runtime dependencies or public contracts.\\n45: \\n46: ## History, attachments, speech and feedback services\\n47: \\n48: These capabilities have built-in defaults and project-owned service wiring.\\n49: Business URLs/auth belong in implementation code, not Agent props. Before connecting a business history, upload, transcription or feedback\\n50: service, read [service configuration](references/service-configuration.md).\\n51: Users supply endpoint/authentication/payload details. Creator edits wiring and uses the confirmed private implementation change tools\\n52: for services/ HTTP integration. Explain operation and shared impact before\\n53: confirmation; public Service contract changes use their separate tools. Report\\n54: applied changes and verification accurately. Preserve explicit custom adapters and Plugin layout.\\n55: \\n56: ## Frontend Tool execution and replay contract\\n57: \\n58: For capability or renderer integration details, read the packaged\\n59: [Frontend Tool lifecycle reference](references/frontend-tool-lifecycle.md).\\n60: \\n61: 1. Product side effects belong only in Tool `execute`: open dialog, navigate,\\n62:    select item, change editor file, modify application state, write storage, copy\\n63:    clipboard, download, call mutation API, submit form, or trigger a host action.\\n64: 2. A Tool renderer is a replayable projection. Assume history load, thread/branch\\n65:    switch, remount, React StrictMode and virtualization can mount it again.\\n66:    `render / mount useEffect != tool invocation`.\\n67: 3. Never implement Agent behavior with\\n68:    `useEffect(() => capability.doSomething(), [])`. Instead use\\n69:    `execute -> capability.doSomething()` and `render -> args/result/status`.\\n70:    UI measurement, ResizeObserver, subscription cleanup, focus and animation\\n71:    effects remain legitimate.\\n72: 4. Results must carry a historical receipt sufficient to render args/result/status\\n73:    without checking today's application state. An opened dialog result can contain\\n74:    `{ opened: true, title: \\\"Settings\\\" }`; history does not require it to stay open.\\n75: 5. History restoration must never execute Frontend Tools. An execute on history\\n76:    load is a Runtime bug; do not patch it with renderer `isHistory` workarounds.\\n77: 6. Keep `Plugin -> Service`, `Application agent-tools -> allowed operation`,\\n78:    `Runtime adapter -> Agent UI native frontend execute`. Plugins never\\n79:    register Tools. Preserve root-only execution scope until subagent attribution\\n80:    has its own contract. Do not add another TOOL_CALL executor or CUSTOM protocol.\\n81: \\n82: ## Official integrations\\n83: \\n84: When Agent UI provides a specialized integration:\\n85: \\n86: 1. Inspect the official integration at the project's pinned upstream revision first.\\n87: 2. Reuse its public semantic contracts where possible.\\n88: 3. Do not bypass AgentUICreator application permission.\\n89: 4. Model reusable adaptation as an Integration, not as a Demo Plugin.\\n90: \\n91: React Hook Form: reuse `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/form-tools.formTools` through\\n92: `integration/react-hook-form`. Use ordinary RHF `useForm()` plus an application\\n93: Service and `createReactHookFormFrontendTools({ serviceName, fields, expose })`.\\n94: `expose` is mandatory and has no default. Do not call `useAssistantForm()` or\\n95: `aui.modelContext.register(...)`; combining those with the factory creates duplicate\\n96: registration and bypasses application permission.\\n97: \\n98: Integration installation != automatic Agent permission. Only application\\n99: frontend-tool modules included in the generated allowlist expose operations.\\n100: Integrations can be pluginless and tool-less. Demo consumers own UI, field names,\\n101: Service lifecycle and replayable receipts; the Integration owns reusable contracts.\\n102: \\n103: ## Agent UI Generative UI\\n104: \\n105: `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/generative-ui` is the canonical component vocabulary and\\n106: renderer. Prefer official vocabulary before creating custom components. Inspect\\n107: the project's installed version: do not infer capabilities from unreleased\\n108: source or from the presence of related CSS selectors.\\n109: \\n110: - Install optional `integration/generative-ui` for the shared factory, official\\n111:   styled vocabulary, Action Registry and public component export. These files\\n112:   are managed together as one optional Integration, not a default Foundation.\\n113: - `createAgentUIGenerativeUI` exposes `present()` and `promptUser()` capabilities.\\n114:   Capability availability does not grant Agent permission. Do not automatically\\n115:   mount or advertise `present` / `prompt_user`; explicit Tool authorization requires a separate product decision.\\n116: - Use the pinned package types and official Form `$input` collection; do not infer availability from CSS selectors.\\n117: - Keep optional source provenance and official styles under Source Registry;\\n118:   regenerate from the pinned package-owned upstream source. CSS permits only\\n119:   mechanical `.agent-ui-conversation` selector scoping, with no visual redesign.\\n120: \\n121: ## Localization ownership\\n122: \\n123: Vendor-owned code must remain locale-agnostic and unmodified; all product\\n124: localization is owned by AgentUICreator composition, adapters, plugins, and Host\\n125: UI. Reuse existing official domain copy through `useAgentUILocale(namespace)`;\\n126: `Agent.locales` overrides only existing keys, not arbitrary new namespaces. For\\n127: new business copy, reuse or create a project-owned shared messages module with\\n128: domain namespaces and complete `en-US` / `zh-CN` parity. Select messages there\\n129: with the unified `useAgentUILocaleCode()` value and pass messages to presentation\\n130: helpers; do not create per-Plugin i18n hooks, language branches or another locale\\n131: state. See the packaged Plugin API's localization example. Preserve protocol IDs, enums, schema and persisted fields;\\n132: locale is a view concern. Prefer public props/composition, then owned adapters.\\n133: If upstream lacks a seam, record a localization gap instead of modifying vendor.\\n134: Include `pnpm check:i18n` where available and check new copy during upgrades.\\n135: \",\"hash\":\"6b4b6349d8b3e3864a1f1581b7dad4a8f5ee1b85202ba5ff1ba7aeced3fef483\",\"totalLines\":135,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_0082cff0c1b44f1e9c2a6a33","toolCallName":"read_file"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_0082cff0c1b44f1e9c2a6a33","delta":"{\"file_path\":\"/skills/ui-layout/SKILL.md\",\"limit\":200,\"offset\":0}"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_END","toolCallId":"call_0082cff0c1b44f1e9c2a6a33"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_0082cff0c1b44f1e9c2a6a33","messageId":"7e054601-d325-499a-bd93-1cd6009003f3","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ui-layout/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ui-layout\\n3: description: Use for Layout Tree decisions involving Row, Column, Stack, Panel, Slot, Sidebar, dimensions, resizing, placement, and composition of existing plugin nodes.\\n4: compatibility: Agent UI Plugin Creator AppUIModel and deterministic compiler boundary.\\n5: allowed-tools: enable_creator_tools read_file ls glob grep inspect_ui_project inspect_app_ui_model inspect_ui_slots list_ui_plugins inspect_ui_plugin mutate_app_ui_model execute\\n6: ---\\n7: \\n8: # UI Layout\\n9: \\n10: Express composition through the AppUIModel Layout Tree rather than DOM manipulation or ad-hoc Plugin CSS.\\n11: \\n12: Do not load this Skill for an eligible `insert_plugin_default` request from a\\n13: fresh Composition Snapshot. The Host resolves the declared authoring placement,\\n14: size, track materialization, and low-level lowering atomically. Load this Skill\\n15: when the semantic operation is unavailable, fails closed, or the user requests\\n16: custom Layout placement or geometry.\\n17: \\n18: ## Node semantics\\n19: \\n20: - `sidebar`: wraps the main `content` and ordered single-plugin navigation `items`.\\n21: - `row`: lays out children horizontally. Optional `sizes` correspond by index to `children`.\\n22: - `column`: lays out children vertically. Optional `sizes` correspond by index to `children`.\\n23: - `stack`: overlays or switches among children; `activeIndex` selects a direct child by index.\\n24: - `panel`: wraps one child and may define width, height, min/max width, or resizing.\\n25: - `slot`: is a physical structural location with an ordered `plugins` array. It has no persisted id, description, hint, or Runtime slot id.\\n26: \\n27: ## Layout rules\\n28: \\n29: - Preserve existing child order and regions unless the request says otherwise.\\n30: - Use the observed `nodeRef` only for the current mutation snapshot. Store each visual plugin directly in the Slot where it appears.\\n31: - For a fixed right region, use a Row with the main content first and a right Panel second.\\n32: - Left/right position and ordering are always Layout Tree changes. Move the existing\\n33:   Plugin instance or its enclosing Panel through `mutate_app_ui_model`; never use\\n34:   `flex-direction`, `order`, transforms or absolute positioning to reverse regions.\\n35:   Move a navigation Panel together with its identity Header and history when the\\n36:   request concerns that whole region. Preserve Plugin identities and child Slots.\\n37: - Platform presets use an explicit Row of navigation/content Panels. Optional\\n38:   left/center/right regions are materialized only when occupied; do not add blank\\n39:   fixed-width columns. Generic Sidebar remains supported for existing layouts.\\n40: - `row.responsive: { type: \\\"drawer\\\", primaryIndex, drawerIndex, minPrimaryWidth }`\\n41:   identifies the main child and the collapsible navigation child. Its drawer opens\\n42:   on the side implied by child order. Moving a Panel preserves those child\\n43:   identities and updates their indices. The legacy `trailing-drawer` policy still\\n44:   requires a drawer after the primary child. When explicitly replacing a Row,\\n45:   declare indices matching the final children.\\n46: - When a Row `sizes` entry and a child Panel `width` describe the same fixed dimension, update both consistently.\\n47: - For Row and Column layouts, the parent `sizes` owns the grid tracks. A fixed-size child Panel does not shrink its parent track. For a fixed sidebar, set the parent tracks to `[W, minmax(0, 1fr)]` and use the matching child Panel width when that authoring constraint is also needed. `gap` only controls space between tracks; inspect the parent tracks and child bounds before attributing a visible layout issue to `gap`.\\n48: - Creator Row/Column track sizes always use explicit CSS strings. Correct values\\n49:   include `\\\"280px\\\"`, `\\\"1fr\\\"`, and `\\\"minmax(0, 1fr)\\\"`; never send numeric\\n50:   track sizes through Creator mutation operations. Runtime numeric Row/Column\\n51:   sizes represent fractional tracks, not pixels. A fixed sidebar example is\\n52:   `[\\\"280px\\\", \\\"minmax(0, 1fr)\\\"]`.\\n53: - When inserting into a Row or Column that already has `sizes`, pass the new\\n54:   child's `size` on `insert_layout_node`, `move_layout_node`, or\\n55:   `insert_layout_relative`; a later `update_layout_node_props` cannot rescue an\\n56:   earlier sequential operation that already failed. When\\n57:   `insert_layout_relative` creates a new sized wrapper, pass `size` and\\n58:   `anchorSize` together.\\n59: - Use a nested Column for vertical subdivision and Stack only when overlapping or active-view behavior is intended.\\n60: - Do not add a plugin node until you have confirmed the referenced Plugin exists.\\n61: - Do not edit Runtime layout code for an application-specific arrangement.\\n62: \\n63: For a pure Layout change, start from\\n64: `inspect_ui_project({\\\"view\\\":\\\"composition\\\"})`; do not re-read AppUIModel,\\n65: manifests, Plugin source, CSS, Services, or generated files when that fresh\\n66: snapshot already contains the needed refs, sizes, Slots, instances, capability\\n67: summaries, authoring placement/size guidance, Service readiness, Host\\n68: guarantees, and constraints. Treat authoring placement and size as defaults:\\n69: explicit user intent and the current Composition take precedence. Apply the\\n70: change through `mutate_app_ui_model` with\\n71: the exact inspected hash. Prefer one batch, use `insert_layout_relative` for\\n72: deterministic left/right/above/below placement, and use `$localRef` when a later\\n73: operation must reference a node created in the same transaction.\\n74: \\n75: ## Generic Sidebar\\n76: \\n77: `sidebar` is a Layout container, not a Plugin. It has `defaultActive: null | itemId`,\\n78: ordered `items: [{ id, child: { type: \\\"slot\\\", plugins: [oneVisualPlugin] } }]`, and\\n79: `content` containing the ordinary Layout Tree. Never store icons, labels or live\\n80: collapse state in AppUIModel. The selected Plugin manifest must declare `sidebar`\\n81: with a supported Lucide icon and optional `en-US`/`zh-CN` labels (Plugin name fallback).\\n82: Supported icons: `messages-square`, `folder`, `folder-open`, `files`, `search`,\\n83: `settings`, `database`, `chart-no-axes-combined`, `list`, `bot`, `book-open`, `star`,\\n84: `circle-help`. Inspect the manifest before choosing a Sidebar placement; edit\\n85: Plugin navigation metadata only when the user asks to change its icon or label.\\n86: \\n87: Use deterministic `mutate_app_ui_model` operations:\\n88: \\n89: - `insert_sidebar_item`: `sidebarRef`, unique `itemId`, optional `index`, and\\n90:   exactly one of `plugin` (new instance) or `instanceId` (move existing instance).\\n91: - `remove_sidebar_item`: `sidebarRef`, `itemId`. Removes its composition entry;\\n92:   preserves Plugin source. Moving out instead uses `move_plugin` to an observed\\n93:   ordinary `layout_slot` or `plugin_slot`; the empty Sidebar item is pruned.\\n94: - `reorder_sidebar_items`: `sidebarRef`, `itemIds` containing every item exactly once.\\n95: - `update_layout_node_props`: `nodeRef`, `set: { defaultActive: null | itemId }`.\\n96: \\n97: These operations participate in the existing atomic transaction and final\\n98: composition validation. For a project without Sidebar, use `replace_layout_node`\\n99: with a complete Sidebar container preserving existing Plugin instances in its\\n100: `content` and moving selected instances into its items. Do not infer tree changes\\n101: from strings or DOM selectors. Desktop rail is 48px with a 280px active panel;\\n102: containers narrower than 648px use a scoped Sheet. Sidebar is available for\\n103: explicit navigation-container composition; preserve existing project layouts.\\n104: \\n105: ### Optional Sidebar Header\\n106: \\n107: `sidebar.header` is an optional ordinary Layout Slot containing at most one visual\\n108: Plugin (zero is valid after removal). It needs no manifest navigation metadata.\\n109: Inspect its existing Slot ref and use `insert_plugin`, `remove_plugin`,\\n110: `replace_plugin`, `move_plugin`, or enable/disable operations just as for any\\n111: Layout Slot. `replace_layout_node` can declare an optional Header Slot, including\\n112: a transaction-local ref; there is no Header-specific install operation.\\n113: \\n114: For Agent name, logo, description or branding requests, reuse `agent-identity`\\n115: and edit `plugins/agent-identity/config.ts`. Its localized defaults use the\\n116: `agentIdentity` namespace; custom brand content need not be translated. Do not\\n117: edit SidebarFrame, vendor components or create a Service to change identity.\\n118: Legacy platform Sidebar presets select history by default; new platform presets\\n119: express navigation as ordinary Row/Panel/Slot composition.\\n120: Models without Header retain the legacy navigation rail. A Header with one\\n121: enabled navigation item uses a single column without a duplicate history icon.\\n122: \",\"hash\":\"a90ae661af2d28b386c251bab64c08fb02fd2257bc1f928f7b525bd014ae070f\",\"totalLines\":122,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

